Progressive fatigue/lethargy, Peripheral pitting oedema, Unexplained nausea/pruritus, eGFR <60 mL/min for >3 months

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GRM-009
Reason for Visit: Severe worsening of generalized peripheral oedema and extreme lethargy, requiring urgent evaluation for acute-on-chronic fluid overload and severe uraemia.
Location: Nursing home
Present at Visit: Patient and nursing staff
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with known declining renal function who has acutely developed severe lower limb swelling, unrelenting nausea, and profound fatigue, rendering them bedbound. Urgent after-hours assessment was mandated to evaluate for impending pulmonary oedema, unmanageable uraemia, and severe uncontrolled hypertension, conditions that risk rapid cardiovascular collapse if unaddressed before the next routine clinical availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My legs are so swollen they feel like they are weeping, I can't keep any food down, and I am itching all over."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports an insidious onset of generalized fatigue and profound lethargy over the past few months, which has sharply declined over the last 72 hours. They have developed progressive bilateral peripheral pitting oedema that now extends to the thighs. Unexplained nausea, vomiting, and profound anorexia have prevented meaningful oral intake for two days. They report a generalized pruritus across their entire body, unassociated with any rash, and a metallic taste in their mouth. Nocturia is prominent. Nursing staff note a worsening pallor and the recent onset of restless legs and muscle cramping at night.
Functional Status: Bedbound currently due to extreme lethargy and heavy, oedematous legs.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of skin breakdown from severe oedema; poor nutritional intake.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 175/100 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.5C | RR: 20 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 88 Kg (up 5kg from last month)
General: Chronically ill-appearing, pale, visibly fatigued, and frequently scratching their arms and chest. Uraemic fetor is detectable.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Hypertension noted; no murmurs. Respiratory: Lungs are clear to auscultation currently, but respiratory rate is borderline elevated. Abdomen: Soft, non-tender. Extremities: Severe 3+ pitting oedema bilaterally extending to the mid-thighs, with tight, shiny skin. Expected investigation results would include a persistently estimated glomerular filtration rate less than 60 mL/min/1.73 m2 for greater than three months alongside progressive normocytic normochromic anaemia.
Environmental Observations: Jug of water by the bed is full, untouched due to nausea. Skin barrier creams present but underutilized.
Medication Reconciliation: Currently prescribed ramipril and amlodipine. Diuretic therapy appears inadequate for the current fluid status.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chronic Kidney Disease - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Congestive Heart Failure Exacerbation - Ruled out/unlikely as the primary driver, given the prominent uraemic symptoms (pruritus, metallic taste, nausea) which point distinctly to advanced renal failure over a purely cardiac event.

Liver Cirrhosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of ascites, jaundice, or stigmata of chronic liver disease, and the specific presence of uraemic fetor.

Acute Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the symmetrical, bilateral nature of the severe pitting oedema.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

This patient demonstrates a severe, acute decompensation of chronic kidney disease characterized by significant fluid overload (hypertension, massive peripheral oedema) and clinical uraemia (intractable nausea, pruritus, metallic taste). The rapid escalation of fluid retention places the patient at imminent risk for flash pulmonary oedema and hypertensive crisis. An urgent after-hours physical examination was essential to assess respiratory reserve, optimize diuretic therapy, and determine if emergency hospital transfer for urgent dialysis was immediately indicated. Delaying this assessment would have constituted a failure to recognize a potentially fatal metabolic and fluid derangement.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered IV Furosemide 40mg stat to aggressively manage the acute fluid overload. Prescribed an antiemetic (Metoclopramide 10mg) to control uraemic nausea. Ordered urgent morning bloods (UECs, CMP, FBC) to accurately quantify the eGFR and potassium levels.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the severe pruritus without causing skin damage, the patient is instructed to use a cold, damp washcloth to press on itchy areas rather than scratching with fingernails. Experiencing immediate, soothing relief from the cold compress will positively reinforce this non-destructive method of symptom control.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Nursing staff instructed to commence strict fluid balance charting and daily weights.
DME/Safety (Optional): Pressure-relieving mattress requested to prevent skin breakdown over oedematous areas.
Follow-up: GP review tomorrow afternoon to check blood results and assess the response to the intravenous diuretic.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department is required if the patient develops shortness of breath while resting, severe chest pain, or a sudden decrease in level of consciousness.

